Best practice: Keep logic minimal within the Jenkinsfile; delegate heavy tasks to scripts (e.g., shell, Python, or Makefiles). Use the 'post' block for notifications and cleanup to ensure visibility and resource efficiency. Always implement fail-fast strategies and utilize shared libraries for reusable logic across pipelines.

pipeline {
agent any
stages {
stage('Build') {
steps { sh 'mvn clean package' }
}
stage('Test') {
steps { sh 'mvn test' }
}
stage('Deploy') {
steps { sh './deploy.sh' }
}
}
post {
always { cleanWs() }
failure { echo 'Alert: Pipeline failed' }
}
}

Best practices: Use declarative syntax for readability, implement ephemeral agents (Docker) for environment isolation, and use 'cleanWs()' to prevent workspace bloat. Always externalize credentials using the Jenkins Credentials Provider rather than hardcoding secrets.

For production scripts, use a `set()` to achieve O(n) time complexity. Avoid loops or list comprehension if performance on large datasets is a concern.

